<p class='text'>I hate open loops.</p>
<p class='text'>I don't actually know if "open loops" is a well defined term or just one that my colleagues and I use based on formal systems theory, so I'll define it in the way I use it. An "open" loop is where I need something, so I've asked someone for "it" and I am waiting for a response.</p>
<p class='text'>The number of reasons I hate them are probably infinite, but I'll cover the most important here.</p>
<p class='text'>Until they are closed, or resolved, they are always there, at the back of my mind, preying on me. I have talked to people who say that, no, once they have fired off a question, they don't think about it again (and presumably never follow up if they don't hear back). My brain only has a limited amount of space, so each of these is living rent-free until it's closed.</p>
<p class='text'>When they do crop up in my mind, the first question that comes to mind is "should I follow up? If so, how?". How long is acceptable to wait before asking or, on the other hand, how long can I wait before I have to conclude it isn't happening. I'm sure you've had this experience yourself. If you are in a restaurant and you have ordered a drink or a starter, and after five minutes it hasn't come, do you risk looking impatient by asking, or do you risk it not coming by waiting? In some restaurants, they now come by and check in frequently, to give you the opportunity to raise these kinds of questions.</p>
<p class='text'>There are other problems too: in general, I want the answer to a question so that I can act on it. In this case, I can't make any progress without an answer. As a generally impatient individual, that annoys me. Given that I also find "context switching" expensive, it annoys me if I have to go and "do something else" for fifteen minutes while I wait (unless, of course, that is coffee and cake).</p>
<p class='text'>As with anything else, of course, you learn over time how other people will behave and I have a whole encyclopedia in my head from "if it's more than an hour, I should probably ask for a welfare check" to "well, I asked, but nothing will happen, so I'll ask again in an hour", along with a whole table of escalations from sending messages to calling to going to their office and sitting there until they answer.</p>
<p class='text'>It's not all about questions though. All sorts of other things control whether or not I can make progress on something. For example, every year as it comes near to the summer, I want to make plans but can't do so reliably until I know what the football schedule is for the coming year.</p>
<p class='text'>And then even wider than that, I have been "writing" this article over the course of three days. Before that, it was on a list for about six months. Before that, it was probably at the back of my mind for a couple of years. All that time it was an open loop and will remain so until I finally push "publish". By the time you read this, the loop will have been closed.</p>
<p class='text'>Lists (and other such strategies) help, of course: once I have put something on a list, it is at least out of my head. It's certainly not "closed" but it is at least parked and I'm not thinking about it. Of course, I do need to then take on the overhead of managing lists, but that's a separate complaint.</p>
